// FourBitAdder.pseudo  (implementation)
//
// ADD YOUR CODE.
//
// This is where the adder is actually assembled, and where every intermediate
// class in the hierarchy belongs as well. Depending on your language you may
// split these across several files in this same intern/ directory - that is
// fine and encouraged; keep one class per file where your language prefers it.
//
// The classes you still owe (handout, section 5):
//
//   AbstractDevice   the abstract root: setting any input recomputes outputs
//   Xor2             NOT + NOT + AND + AND + OR, built from primitives
//   HalfAdder        1x Xor2 + 1x AND               -> Sum, Carry
//   FullAdder        2x HalfAdder + 1x OR           -> Sum, Cout
//   FourBitAdder     4x FullAdder, ripple carry     -> Sum[0..3], Cout
//
// Two things worth deciding early, because they shape everything above them:
//
//   1. How a composite device propagates change. When an input pin is set on
//      the adder, the signal has to reach every gate it feeds, in dependency
//      order, before any output is read. Solve this once in AbstractDevice
//      rather than in each composite.
//
//   2. Whether your composition generalizes. A four-bit adder can be written
//      as four copy-pasted stanzas or as one composition applied four times.
//      Both pass the same tests. They are not equally good answers, and the
//      difference will matter more than you expect.
//
// Remember the hard rule: real boolean logic appears ONLY inside the update()
// of AND, OR, and NAND. Everything here is composition and wiring.
